    diagram: ["local     — one call only; parameters are COPIES (pass by value)", "global    — whole program, visible everywhere; shared-state risk", "static    — whole program lifetime, one function's visibility", "write     — a function changes your variable only via its address (&)"],
    terms: [
      { term: "Pass by value", def: "The function receives a copy; the caller's variable cannot change." },
      { term: "Prototype", def: "A function declaration (signature) before use, enabling type-checked calls." },
      { term: "static local", def: "A local variable that keeps its value between calls." },
      { term: "Stack frame", def: "One call's storage on the stack — recursion depth is frame count." },
    ],
    code: { lang: "c", title: "scope.c — complete, runnable (C11)", code: "#include <stdio.h>\n\nint threshold = 5;                 /* global: one copy, visible everywhere */\n\nint judge(int attempts) {          /* parameter is a COPY (pass by value) */\n    attempts += 1;                 /* changes only the local copy */\n    return attempts >= threshold ? 1 : 0;\n}\n\nint counter(void) {\n    static int calls = 0;          /* static local: survives between calls */\n    calls++;\n    return calls;\n}\n\nlong factorial(int n) {\n    if (n <= 1) return 1;          /* base case */\n    return n * factorial(n - 1);   /* recursion: smaller problem each time */\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int attempts = 4;\n    printf(\"judge(4)=%d, attempts still %d\\n\", judge(attempts), attempts);\n    int c1 = counter(), c2 = counter(), c3 = counter();\n    printf(\"counter calls return: %d %d %d\\n\", c1, c2, c3);\n    printf(\"factorial(5)=%ld\\n\", factorial(5));\n    return 0;\n}", output: "judge(4)=1, attempts still 4\ncounter calls return: 1 2 3\nfactorial(5)=120", lines: ["judge incremented its COPY to 5 and returned 1; main's attempts prints 4 — pass by value, proven.", "The three counter() results are stored BEFORE printing — argument evaluation order in one printf is unspecified in C, a real gotcha this code avoids on purpose.", "static calls initialises ONCE (first call), then persists — memory without exposure.", "factorial's base case at n <= 1 is what makes the recursion a staircase instead of a cliff."] },
    mistakes: ["Expecting a function to update your variable without passing & — then 'fixing' it with a global.", "Making everything global to dodge parameter design — shared state with no owner.", "Recursion without a reachable base case: stack overflow, in C a crash, not an exception."],
    security: "Globals holding sensitive state (a 'current user' variable) are a classic confused-deputy source; static buffers returned from functions are shared across callers. Scope is an access-control decision.",
    pc: ["Compile scope.c; add a fourth counter() call stored the same way and predict the line.", "Write swap(int a, int b) that 'works' locally, print the caller's values, and explain the result — then fix it with pointers."],
    android: ["Android: identical C semantics in Termux/clang."],
    lab: "Guided lab: in the Playground, write bump(int *p) that increments the caller's counter through the pointer, and prove the caller's variable changed.",
    mission: "Mission: explain pass by value using the judge output (1 and 4) to a classmate in two sentences.",
    practical: "Practical assessment: predict caller-variable outcomes, choose the right storage class, and spot the missing base case.",
    quiz: [
      { q: "After judge(4) returns 1, main's attempts is still 4 because:", options: ["judge failed", "C passes by value — judge incremented its own copy", "printf cached it", "threshold blocked it"], answer: 1, why: "The parameter is a copy; only an address (&attempts) grants write access." },
      { q: "A function needs a private counter that persists across calls. The tool:", options: ["A global", "A static local — program lifetime, function-only visibility", "A parameter", "A macro"], answer: 1, why: "static local is exactly 'remembers, but stays private'." },
      { q: "factorial without the n <= 1 base case:", options: ["Returns 0", "Recurses until the stack overflows and the program crashes", "Loops twice", "Returns n"], answer: 1, why: "Every call adds a stack frame; no base case means no bottom." },
    ],
    summary: "Pass by value (pointers grant write access), locals/globals/static lifetimes, prototypes, and recursion with a base case. Next: typedef, enum, and union.",
    skillGained: "C function semantics, scope, and storage classes.",
